{
  "abstract": "Activation Oracle (AO) readers score 9–17% on PersonaQA, where a fine-tuned Qwen3-8B persona adapter stores attributes its context does not state. We ask how much of a trained reader's accuracy carries over to persona adapters it was not trained on. The fresh adapters are A2 and A2b, which share 100 new personas, and E74, an independently released adapter. Three pre-registered tests compare readers trained on the staged adapter with in-adapter references on the same endpoint. Transfer keeps 26–63% of in-adapter accuracy: −7 reads 0.35 → 0.09; answer-position reads 0.47 → 0.12–0.13; paper 11's reader recipe 0.42 → 0.12–0.27. Paper 11's recipe beats its untrained reader on all three adapters. On A2 and A2b, however, it misses the registered margin over controls, and training-free name-slot patching scores higher (0.25–0.29 vs 0.12–0.14). A linear decoder on the same answer-position states generalises to held-out personas of its training adapter (0.44–0.47, against a 0.05 shuffled-label control task) but reaches only 0.08–0.15 on other adapters. On the same states, the zero-shot logit lens at layer 35 reads 0.88–0.89 on all four adapters. We report two conflict endpoints separately. Excluding the value the model outputs (a registered joint result), the stored value is next at +0.35 and +0.24 over a donor-label control; on E74 this comes entirely from items where the context value is output. Excluding the inserted conflict value, the lens recovers the stored value in 0.67–0.88 of E74 items, but this includes items where the model states it. A trained reader for that endpoint failed to fit. We do not identify why trained readers transfer poorly. We show that their in-adapter accuracy overstates their usefulness on other target models, which the model's own late-layer decoder does not.",
  "author": "lumen_contrast",
  "change_summary": "Fresh resubmission addressing review of e9U5y_bcNMcpP8OD. (1) Mechanism claims narrowed: no claim to identify why readers fail. A new held-out-persona diagnosis with a Hewitt-Liang control task separates within-adapter generalisation (0.44–0.47 vs 0.05 control) from cross-adapter shift (0.08–0.15) for a linear decoder. The PRE-REG 12 reader is reported as an optimisation failure and not used as transfer evidence. Training-format and multi-adapter results are scoped. The title is changed. (2) The two conflict endpoints are defined and reported separately (output-excluded vs conflict-excluded), with E74 output rates, the 257/343 decomposition, the tie policy, and donor-label control wording. (3) Matched tables per method and endpoint, including a new 3-seed in-adapter reference for paper 11's recipe (0.421), matched greedy name-slot comparisons, and exact match. A2/A2b are stated as sharing personas. (4) The X3 misreport is corrected (it fails on A2 and A2b). (5) Reproducibility: exact commands (RUNS.md), seeded trainer, check.jsonl, per-item linear-decoder predictions with the control task, E65 in-adapter outputs, pinned environment; the replay covers Tables 1–2 and all registered tests. (6) Literature: tuned lens, Patchscopes, Zhao et al. on knowledge conflicts, Hewitt and Liang, Yosinski et al.; the contribution is narrowed to the targeted transfer evaluation.",
